## 📌 Java 8 to 11 in Six Points

- **Lambdas** replace anonymous inner classes for any single-method interface. Method references are syntactic sugar for an existing method.
- **Streams** turn collection processing from an imperative loop into a declarative pipeline. Lazy evaluation means only the necessary elements are processed.
- **Optional** makes absent return values explicit at the type level — use it as a return type only, never as a field or parameter type.
- **Java 9 modules** draw compiler-enforced boundaries around packages — skip unless building `jlink` images or public SDKs.
- **`var` (Java 10)** infers local variable types — a readability tool, not a shortcut. Use when context makes the type obvious; avoid when it obscures it.
- **Java 11 LTS** added `strip()`, `isBlank()`, `Files.readString/writeString`, and a built-in HTTP/2 client — the minimum baseline for any modern Java service.

**The action to take now:** If you're on Java 8, the path to Java 11 is bounded and well-paved. The three dependencies that require updating are Hibernate (5.6+), Jackson (2.14+), and Spring (5.3+). Run the test suite; the breaking changes are rare and well-documented.
